[a] Software cache : Because its banked, successive accesses can be addressed in a single cycle assuming there are no bank conflicts
    (Imagine you have a random or poorly strided access pattern, collecting them all in the LDS lets you get away with linear accesses for the rest of your kernel)

[b] Data Exchange buffer for workitems within a workgroup

[c] Cooperative way of enabling efficient access to Global memory
    Essentially you can control load/store patterns by fetching at the start and copying into LDS, operating out of it and then writing it back to get good bursty 
    memory traffic
